  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $a\\ge b\\ge c\\ge d\\ge e\\ge f$ are natural numbers for which\n$$ad+ae+af+bd+be+bf+cd+ce+cf=21.$$\nFind their values.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,b,c,d,e,f)=(3,2,2,1,1,1),\\ (3,3,1,1,1,1),\\ (4,2,1,1,1,1),\\ (5,1,1,1,1,1)$.\n\nGroup the terms to obtain $(a+b+c)(d+e+f)=21$.",
      "worked": "Grouping gives\n$$\\begin{align*}ad+ae+af+bd+be+bf+cd+ce+cf&=a(d+e+f)+b(d+e+f)+c(d+e+f)\\\\ad+ae+af+bd+be+bf+cd+ce+cf&=(a+b+c)(d+e+f).\n\\end{align*}$$\nBoth sums are at least $3$, and $a+b+c\\ge d+e+f$. The only possible factor pair is therefore $7\\times3$.\n$$d+e+f=3,\\qquad a+b+c=7.$$\nThus $d=e=f=1$. The non-increasing positive triples summing to $7$ are $(3,2,2)$, $(3,3,1)$, $(4,2,1)$ and $(5,1,1)$.\nHence\n$$(a,b,c,d,e,f)=(3,2,2,1,1,1),\\ (3,3,1,1,1,1),\\ (4,2,1,1,1,1),\\ (5,1,1,1,1,1).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
